  "query_preview": "..Mostly dry conditions are expected across the area\nthrough next week. \n\nThrough Sunday...The upper low that moved through the region on \nFriday has settled over Mexico and continues to move towards the \nsouth and east. In its wake we have northeasterly flow across the \narea with mild temperatures and breezy conditions. There is still \nsome lingering moisture around but no precipitation is expected. \nSome fog may develop again tonight, mainly across eastern Arizona \nand along the Mogollon Rim. While winds should decouple and drop off \nthis evening, some gusts to 25 mph will continue overnight in areas \ndirectly south/southwest of the higher terrain. \n\nMonday through Thursday...The pattern through much of the week \nlooks to remain mostly dry. A weak trough will cross near or just \nsouth of southern Arizona on Tuesday, but the system lacks strength\nand moisture, which means we will likely remain dry across northern\nArizona. Should a little bit of moisture sneak in on the eastern \nedge of the trough in the weak southeast flow, it would result in \nonly an isolated shower or two, mainly confined to the White \nMountains. Expect temperatures to remain mild through the period \nwith generally light winds. \n\nFriday through next weekend...Looking towards the end of the week, \nthe models continue to show a deepening west coast trough that \nbecomes a cut-off low as it moves towards Baja California. While the \npast few days the models have trended south with the low, the most \nrecent operational runs have shifts the low slightly to the north. \nAdditionally, they weaken the low as it moves towards the area and \nthen lift it to the east/northeast over the weekend. At this point \nin time we may see some showers and gusty winds across the area over \nthe weekend, but confidence is low as to just how impactful this low \nwill be due to timing, position, and strength of the low. With this \nbeing at the very end of the forecast period, it bears watching \nsince we have seen a very active pattern across the region and we \nwill need to monitor this to see how the trends line up over the \nnext few days.",
